{
  "teamId": 140,
  "year": 2019,
  "narrative": "The 2019 Texas Rangers opened the season with a burst of electricity that briefly disguised the mediocre club they would prove to be. Joey Gallo's opening-weekend three-run blast and Delino DeShields' grand slam announced a team capable of big offensive moments, and a 2-1 March gave way to a competitive April. But the pitching staff — headlined by a bloated 5.06 ERA — was always lurking as the hidden flaw, with closer José Leclerc blowing crucial leads and the bullpen routinely squandering hard-fought positions.\n\nThe Rangers found their best baseball in June, going a remarkable 18-11 to crest above .500 and briefly flirt with relevance in the AL West race. Nomar Mazara's clutch hits, Danny Santana's surprising emergence as a multi-tool weapon, and a string of bullpen double plays in Boston hinted at a team that could string together winning stretches. Willie Calhoun, Hunter Pence, and Rougned Odor all delivered timely blows to keep the ship afloat through May and into June's hot run.\n\nJuly shattered the illusion. A brutal 8-16 month exposed every structural weakness — a rotation that couldn't sustain leads, a bullpen that surrendered walk-off moments to the Astros and Athletics, and an offense that went cold at the worst times. Brett Martin and José Leclerc absorbed punishing defeats against Houston and Oakland, and the Rangers never recovered their footing. August and September became a exercise in individual highlights — Rougned Odor's 20th and 25th home runs, Nick Solak's energetic debut after being acquired at the trade deadline, Emmanuel Clase's brief audition — set against a backdrop of steady losing.\n\nThe 2019 Rangers finished 78-84, a team with a fun power offense (223 home runs) undermined by pitching that simply could not protect leads. They scored 810 runs yet allowed 878, a run differential that told the whole story. The season ended with Danny Santana's closing grand slam — a fitting symbol of the year: individual fireworks on a team that never quite came together when it mattered most.",
  "chapters": [
